# =============================================================================
# A C/H on the CLEAN NACA 0012, built at the scale GridPro's own C-grid video
# states AND arranged the way that video arranges them: 56 blocks / 3692 cells
# against its 57 / 3648.
#
# From docs/research/nesting-blocks-at-a-complicated-leading-edge.md, which
# records "C-Grid Mesh and Topology in GridPro" (4Jeu-EUXXmo). Its status bar
# reads 57 blocks / 3648 cells -- read off the published frame, section 2 there.
# NOTE that the Grid Quality dialog legible on the same frame belongs to a
# DIFFERENT tutorial (its own File Name field points at a Y-exhaust directory),
# so there is no vendor quality figure for this case to compare against, and
# none is quoted.
#
# WHY 56 AND NOT 57. This generator's block count is 2 * LAYERS * (SPLIT + WAKE)
# -- derived by measuring 18 combinations of the three, exact in every one -- so
# EVERY count it can produce is even and 57 is unreachable by construction, not
# by tuning. Its topology is mirror-symmetric about the wake line, so blocks
# come in top/bottom pairs; GridPro's 57 is odd, so theirs is not. Section 7.
#
# The cell count is reached with --node-scale, which multiplies the node counts
# without touching the blocking. Everything else in this directory runs at 1.0.
#
# HOW THE BLOCKS ARE SPENT, which matching the COUNT does not settle. The first
# version put 56 blocks as --layers 1 --split 22: twenty-two chordwise slivers
# per surface, each running the whole radial span. Put beside the video frame at
# a matched window that is visibly NOT what GridPro does -- its nose is wrapped
# in concentric bands and only about five or six chordwise cuts cross the whole
# view. Spending the same 56 on --layers 2 --split 8 instead is closer to the
# frame AND better on every measure: worst corner 58.49 -> 52.29 deg, mean
# 3.81 -> 3.10. Section 7d.
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py \
#       --layers 2 --split 8 --wake 6 --turn-blend 0.8 --node-scale 0.67 \
#       --out gridpro_ch_video.json
#   ./run.sh -conf docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_video.dat
#
# MB_SPLIT_QUADS 0 HERE, unlike every other case in this directory. GridPro's
# published grids are quad and its 3648 is a quad count, so splitting would
# compare 7276 triangles against 3648 quads. Caught by the shipped default
# reporting exactly twice the swept number.
#
# A RESEARCH HARNESS, not a shipped case.
#
MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_video.json

# The aerofoil the C-grid case already ships: two segments, upper and lower,
# split at the leading edge because that is where a block corner sits. There is
# NO far-field geometry -- the rectangle is declared, corner by corner.
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/naca0012_cgrid.dat

BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001
MB_SPLIT_QUADS 0

# The FALLBACK, and here it is load bearing: the far-field and outlet edges
# carry no binding, so this is where their condition comes from. The aerofoil's
# sides are bound and take 'wall' off their own sidecar. A real case would want
# inlet / outlet / farfield told apart, which needs a far-field geometry file.
BC_GEOM farfield

# The repo default of 20 sweeps DIVERGES on this family (section 8g); five is
# where the wall height is held and nothing has turned.
MB_SMOOTH_ITERS 5

EXPORT_VTK 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/gridpro_ch_video/mesh_gridpro_ch_video.vtk
